---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, WEB_LAG_DAYS } from '../site.config';
import { withBase, paths } from '../lib/url';
---

<Base title="About" description={`What ${site.name} is, how it is written, and the standards it holds itself to.`} path={paths.about()}>
  <div class="narrow prose about">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About Football Stories 101</h1>
    <p>
      Football Stories 101 is a free, non-commercial football storytelling publication. One researched story goes out
      every morning, seven days a week, short enough to read over breakfast, with its own picture. The email is the product;
      this website is the archive, and each story appears here {WEB_LAG_DAYS} days after it was sent.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scope is global: footballers and stars past and present, clubs, and the eras and dynasties that defined the game.
      Stories are written under the byline <em>{site.byline}</em>. There is no advertising, no paywall and no sponsorship.
    </p>

    <h2>The four pillars</h2>
    <ul>
      {Object.entries(site.pillars).map(([key, p]) => (
        <li>
          <a href={withBase(paths.pillar(key))}><strong>{p.label}</strong></a>: {p.blurb}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
    <h3>The two-source rule</h3>
    <p>
      Every factual claim rests on two independent sources, and every story lists its sources in a footer. When reputable
      sources disagree, the story says so rather than picking one quietly. Quotations are short, verbatim and attributed to
      where they were first recorded.
    </p>
    <h3>The living-persons rule</h3>
    <p>
      An allegation about a living person, whether about match-fixing, doping, misconduct or private life, appears only if
      it is an established fact from a reputable primary source such as a conviction, an official finding or the person's
      own admission; only when it is phrased as attributed fact; and only when the story needs it. Otherwise it is left out.
      Where in doubt, the publication prefers documented events and deceased subjects.
    </p>
    <h3>AI-assistance disclosure</h3>
    <p>
      Research, drafting and editing are done with the help of AI tools, under a strict rule: AI assistance is allowed for
      structure and prose, never for facts. Every fact in a published story traces to a source in the research brief that
      the editor assembled and checked. The editor is responsible for every word.
    </p>
    <h3>Images</h3>
    <p>
      The hero image on each story is the publication's own illustration unless credited otherwise. Photographs are used
      only from Wikimedia Commons with author, licence and source credited, from the editor's own camera, or under a paid
      licence. No club crests are used as design elements, and no match footage appears anywhere.
    </p>
    <h3>Style</h3>
    <p>
      Told in the editor's voice, past tense for history, present tense for the reflection at the end. British English. Numerals for
      scores, ages, fees and attendances. Full names on first mention. No exclamation marks.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
    <p>
      Errors are corrected on the web version and logged, with dates, on the <a href={withBase(paths.corrections())}>corrections page</a>.
      For corrections, takedown requests or anything else, email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>
    <p>
      The site is a static build published through GitHub Pages. Search runs in the browser. There are no cookies, no
      third-party share scripts and no tracking pixels. Subscriptions are handled by Buttondown; see the
      <a href={withBase(paths.privacy())}>privacy page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about h1 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .about :global(.eyebrow) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
